---
import DocShell from '../../layouts/doc-shell.astro';
import { docsLinks } from '../../lib/nav';

const toc = [
   { id: 'families', label: 'Command families' },
   { id: 'shared', label: 'Shared options' },
   { id: 'sr', label: 'a1 sr' },
   { id: 'keys', label: 'Key chords' },
   { id: 'wcag', label: 'a1 wcag' },
   { id: 'axe', label: 'a1 axe' },
   { id: 'tree', label: 'a1 tree' },
   { id: 'audit', label: 'a1 audit' },
   { id: 'assessment', label: 'Agent-led assessment' },
   { id: 'doctor', label: 'a1 doctor and mcp' },
   { id: 'browser', label: 'Browser selection' },
];
---

<DocShell
   title="CLI reference"
   summary="Every command, subcommand, and option in the a1 binary. Run a1 help-all to print the whole tree in one response, which is the fastest way to give an agent the full command set."
   links={docsLinks}
   toc={toc}
>
   <section class="ledger" id="families">
      <div class="panel-head">
         <p class="panel-kicker">Map</p>
         <h2>Command families</h2>
      </div>
      <p>
         Most commands check a target against axe-core (axe), an open-source rules engine that
         runs inside the page and flags accessibility problems, or look up the Web Content
         Accessibility Guidelines (WCAG), the standard most accessibility requirements come from.
      </p>
      <div class="route-list">
         <div class="route-row"><code>a1 sr</code><span>Control a screen reader through a session.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>a1 wcag</code><span>Look up criteria, techniques, axe rules, search results, and coverage.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>a1 axe</code><span>Run axe-core against one or more targets.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>a1 tree</code><span>Print a target's accessibility tree.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>a1 audit</code><span>Run axe, the accessibility tree, and applicability together, with a criterion rollup.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>a1 mcp</code><span>Start the stdio MCP server.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>a1 doctor</code><span>Check the host and list the setup steps still needed.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>a1 setup</code><span>Run the Guidepup setup steps this host needs, then re-check.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>a1 help-all</code><span>Print help for every command and option at once.</span></div>
      </div>
   </section>

   <section class="split panel" id="shared">
      <div class="stack">
         <div class="panel-head">
            <p class="panel-kicker">Options</p>
            <h2>Shared options</h2>
         </div>
         <div class="route-list">
            <div class="route-row"><code>--json</code><span>Print one structured object instead of formatted text.</span></div>
            <div class="route-row"><code>--verbose</code><span>Add detail to the text output. No effect with <code>--json</code>.</span></div>
            <div class="route-row"><code>--wcag &lt;version&gt;</code><span>WCAG version for <code>wcag</code>, <code>axe</code>, and <code>audit</code>. Accepts <code>2.1</code> or <code>2.2</code>. Defaults to <code>2.2</code>.</span></div>
         </div>
         <p>
            <code>-V</code> at the top level prints the package version. <code>--wcag</code> inside
            a subcommand selects the WCAG version, so the two no longer share one flag name.
         </p>
      </div>
      <div class="split-aside">
         <div class="tag">Environment</div>
         <p class="aside-note">
            <code>A11IED_STATE_DIR</code> sets the directory that stores the active session's
            state file, lock, and broker socket. Without it, a11ied uses
            <code>$XDG_STATE_HOME/a11ied</code>, then <code>~/.a11ied/state</code>.
            Native CLI sessions always run in a separate broker so cleanup can be retried
            after the command exits. <code>A11IED_DRIVER_MODE=in-process</code> applies to
            virtual sessions and API hosts that remain running.
         </p>
      </div>
   </section>

   <section class="panel" id="sr">
      <div class="panel-head">
         <p class="panel-kicker">Screen reader</p>
         <h2>a1 sr</h2>
      </div>
      <p>
         One session is active at a time. No command takes or prints a session id. A landmark, in
         the <code>next</code> and <code>previous</code> kind list below, is a named region such
         as banner, navigation, or main that a screen reader lets a user jump straight to.
      </p>
      <div class="route-list">
         <div class="route-row"><code>sr start [url]</code><span>Start the session, stopping any active one first. Opens the URL in a browser and waits until its window is in front. Takes <code>--sr</code>, <code>--browser &lt;name&gt;</code>, <code>--app &lt;name&gt;</code> for a native app instead of a page, <code>--recording &lt;path&gt;</code>, <code>--idle-timeout &lt;minutes&gt;</code>.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>sr open &lt;url&gt;</code><span>Move the running session to another page.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>sr status</code><span>Print the session target, URL, uptime, recording, and transcript counts.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>sr stop</code><span>Stop the session and delete its state file. Takes <code>--out &lt;path&gt;</code> and <code>--format</code>.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>sr read</code><span>The current item without moving: role, name, value, states, the phrase, and the source of each field.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>sr title</code><span>The page title: <code>document.title</code>, the VoiceOver window summary, or the NVDA window title.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><div class="route-terms"><code>sr next [kind]</code>, <code>sr previous [kind]</code></div><span>Move one item, or jump by kind: <code>heading</code>, <code>link</code>, <code>landmark</code>, <code>control</code>, <code>button</code>, <code>table</code>, <code>list</code>, <code>graphic</code>, <code>region</code>, <code>form-field</code>. Takes <code>--level &lt;1-6&gt;</code> with heading and <code>--times &lt;n&gt;</code>.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><div class="route-terms"><code>sr top</code>, <code>sr bottom</code></div><span>Move to the start or the end of the document or the current area.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><div class="route-terms"><code>sr interact</code>, <code>sr stop-interacting</code>, <code>sr activate</code></div><span>Enter a group or control, leave it, and activate the current item.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>sr escape</code><span>Press Escape to dismiss a menu or a dialog.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>sr find &lt;text&gt;</code><span>Move the cursor to the next place the text appears. Exits 4 when it is not found. Needs an active session.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>sr table &lt;move&gt;</code><span>Inside a table: <code>next-cell</code>, <code>previous-cell</code>, <code>next-row</code>, <code>previous-row</code>, <code>next-column</code>, <code>previous-column</code>, <code>row-header</code>, <code>column-header</code>.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>sr goto</code><span>Step forward until the current item matches <code>--role &lt;role&gt;</code>, <code>--name &lt;text&gt;</code>, or both. Takes <code>--max</code>. Exits 4 when nothing matches. Needs an active session.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>sr elements &lt;kind&gt;</code><span>The rotor: from the top, every element of one kind as the reader announces it. Takes <code>--max</code>, default 200. Needs an active session.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>sr read-all</code><span>Read from the cursor to the end of the document, or to <code>--max</code> (default 500), and say which. Needs an active session. Use <code>sr walk</code> to read a whole page instead.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>sr walk [url]</code><span>Start a session when none is active, move to the top, read the whole page, and print the transcript of the walk. Takes <code>--sr</code>, <code>--max</code>, <code>--out</code>, <code>--format</code>. With no <code>--sr</code> and no active session, this starts VoiceOver on macOS.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>sr press &lt;chord...&gt;</code><span>Press key chords in order, one chord per argument.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>sr type &lt;text&gt;</code><span>Type text through the active target.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>sr do &lt;command&gt;</code><span>Run a named command, or <code>&lt;command-set&gt;:&lt;name&gt;</code> when two sets share a name. Takes <code>--command-set</code>.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>sr list</code><span>The commands <code>sr do</code> accepts, grouped by command set and by what they do. Takes <code>--query</code>, <code>--sr</code>, <code>--command-set</code>.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>sr focus</code><span>Bring a window to the front. With no options, refocus the app the session opened. Takes <code>--app</code>, <code>--bundle-id</code>, <code>--process</code>, <code>--pid</code>, <code>--window-title</code>, <code>--match</code>.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>sr screenshot &lt;path&gt;</code><span>Save a picture of what the VoiceOver cursor is on. NVDA and virtual exit 2.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>sr wait</code><span>Pause for <code>--ms</code>, or poll the transcript until <code>--for &lt;text|/regex/&gt;</code> is announced. Use <code>--since &lt;checkpoint&gt;</code> to include announcements after a checkpoint. Exits 4 at <code>--timeout</code>, default 5000 ms.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>sr expect &lt;text|/regex/&gt;</code><span>Check the transcript for a phrase. Takes <code>--since &lt;checkpoint&gt;</code> and <code>--not</code>. Exits 4 on a mismatch.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>sr checkpoint &lt;label&gt;</code><span>Mark a point in the transcript for <code>--since</code>.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>sr transcript</code><span>Print what the reader said, with timestamps. Takes <code>--since</code>, <code>--tail</code>, <code>--after-index</code>, <code>--limit</code>, <code>--out</code>, <code>--format</code>. Index paging defaults to 200 entries. The transcript window reports omissions and the next index.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>sr tail</code><span>Stream spoken announcements continuously in real time. Takes <code>--lines &lt;n&gt;</code> (default 10), <code>--since &lt;checkpoint&gt;</code>, <code>--interval &lt;ms&gt;</code> (default 250), <code>--phrase</code> (phrase only), and <code>--json</code> (NDJSON). Aliases: <code>follow</code>, <code>stream</code>, <code>watch</code>.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>sr batch [file]</code><span>Run JSON lines of actions, one per line or from stdin, over one broker connection. Stops at the first failed expect unless <code>--continue</code>.</span></div>
      </div>
      <h3>Session options</h3>
      <p>
         Native sessions default to <code>guarded</code>. Commands check the observed
      foreground target, and typing checks between characters. Read the fresh
      current item, focus observations, and transcript after each action. If focus
      changes, refocus deliberately and inspect state before continuing.
      <code>require-binding</code> is an optional strict mode that refuses unavailable
      native binding. <code>development</code> bypasses target checks. Foreground
      checks do not guarantee that focus cannot change during dispatch.
   </p>
      <div class="route-list">
         <div class="route-row"><code>--sr &lt;reader&gt;</code><span><code>voiceover</code>, <code>nvda</code>, or <code>virtual</code>, on <code>sr start</code>, <code>sr walk</code>, and the commands that start a session. Without it, a11ied picks the platform reader and falls back to virtual.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>--timeout &lt;ms&gt;</code><span>Bound the screen reader command instead of using the built-in limits. On <code>sr wait</code> it is how long to wait for the phrase.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>--phrase</code><span>On a navigation verb, print the last spoken phrase alone, one line.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>--ephemeral</code><span>Run one action and stop its session. Native actions use a detached broker. Unconfirmed cleanup leaves a recovery session.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>--command-set &lt;set&gt;</code><span><code>auto</code>, <code>portable</code>, <code>voiceover-commander</code>, or <code>voiceover-keycode</code>. Defaults to <code>auto</code>.</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="route-list">
         <div class="route-row"><code>0</code><span>The command ran.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>2</code><span>Usage error: a command the target cannot run, or a verb run with no active session.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>3</code><span>The browser or the broker process failed to start.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>4</code><span>A check failed: <code>find</code>, <code>goto</code>, <code>wait --for</code>, <code>expect</code>, or a <code>batch</code> with a failed expect.</span></div>
      </div>
      <p>
         <code>press</code>, <code>type</code>, <code>do</code>, and <code>walk</code> start a
         session when none is active. The other verbs need one and exit 2 without it.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="split panel" id="keys">
      <div class="stack">
         <div class="panel-head">
            <p class="panel-kicker">Keys</p>
            <h2>Key chord syntax</h2>
         </div>
         <p>
            <code>sr press</code> takes one chord per argument, and each chord joins its tokens
            with <code>+</code>: <code>Tab</code>, <code>Shift+Tab</code>,
            <code>VO+ArrowRight</code>, or <code>NVDA+N</code>.
         </p>
         <div class="route-list">
            <div class="route-row"><strong>Modifiers</strong><span><code>Shift</code>, <code>Control</code>, <code>Alt</code></span></div>
            <div class="route-row"><strong>Letters and digits</strong><span><code>a</code> to <code>z</code>, <code>KeyA</code> to <code>KeyZ</code>, <code>0</code> to <code>9</code>, <code>Digit0</code> to <code>Digit9</code></span></div>
            <div class="route-row"><strong>Arrows</strong><span><code>ArrowUp</code>, <code>ArrowDown</code>, <code>ArrowLeft</code>, <code>ArrowRight</code>, and the <code>Up</code> and <code>UpArrow</code> aliases</span></div>
            <div class="route-row"><strong>Navigation</strong><span><code>Tab</code>, <code>Enter</code>, <code>Escape</code>, <code>Space</code>, <code>Backspace</code>, <code>Delete</code>, <code>Home</code>, <code>End</code>, <code>PageUp</code>, <code>PageDown</code></span></div>
            <div class="route-row"><strong>VoiceOver</strong><span><code>VO</code> for Control and Option, plus <code>Command</code>, <code>Option</code>, <code>Meta</code>, and <code>Fn</code></span></div>
            <div class="route-row"><strong>NVDA</strong><span><code>NVDA</code> for Insert, plus <code>Windows</code>, <code>Application</code>, and the <code>NumPad0</code> to <code>NumPad9</code> keys</span></div>
         </div>
         <pre><code>a1 sr press Tab Tab --sr virtual --json
a1 sr press "VO+ArrowRight" --json</code></pre>
      </div>
      <div class="split-aside">
         <div class="tag">Full list</div>
         <p class="aside-note">
            <code>a1 sr press --help</code> prints every token the current platform accepts,
            including function keys and punctuation names. <code>press</code> starts a
            session when none is active, defaulting to VoiceOver on macOS, so pass
            <code>--sr virtual</code> unless a VoiceOver chord such as
            <code>VO+ArrowRight</code> is what you want to run.
         </p>
      </div>
   </section>

   <section class="panel" id="wcag">
      <div class="panel-head">
         <p class="panel-kicker">Standards</p>
         <h2>a1 wcag</h2>
      </div>
      <p>
         A success criterion is one testable WCAG requirement, identified by a number such as
         <code>1.4.3</code> and assigned a conformance level of A, AA, or AAA that says how strict
         it is. This family looks up criteria by that number, by slug, or by technique ID.
      </p>
      <div class="route-list">
         <div class="route-row"><code>wcag</code><span>With no arguments on a terminal, an interactive finder: fuzzy search on the left, the criterion on the right, <code>t</code>, <code>f</code>, <code>u</code>, and <code>a</code> for testing it, if it fails, the Understanding document, and everything, <code>y</code> to copy the <code>a1 axe --criterion</code> command. Piped output prints the help.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>wcag &lt;criterion&gt;</code><span>One criterion by ID or slug, or one technique by ID such as <code>G18</code>. Same output as <code>wcag show</code>.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>wcag criteria</code><span>List criteria. <code>--level A</code>, <code>AA</code>, or <code>AAA</code> narrows it. <code>--summary</code> prints the count of automated, hybrid, and manual criteria per level instead. See <a href="/test-methods">test methods</a>.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>wcag show &lt;criterion&gt;</code><span>The requirement, a plain restatement of what to do, what WCAG2Mobile says about it on a mobile platform, how to test it, and what to do when it fails. <code>--verbose</code> adds the raw <code>testMethod.method</code>, <code>preferredEvidenceMode</code>, procedure IDs, and the rest of the mobile guidance.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>wcag understanding &lt;criterion&gt;</code><span>The full Understanding document for one criterion, converted to Markdown, with the source line the W3C Document License requires.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>wcag rule &lt;ruleId&gt;</code><span>One axe-core rule: the criteria it maps to, and the axe help text and URL.</span></div>
      </div>
      <pre><code>a1 wcag 1.4.3
a1 wcag show status-messages --json
a1 wcag understanding status-messages
a1 wcag criteria --level AA --wcag 2.1 --json
a1 wcag criteria --summary
a1 wcag rule color-contrast
a1 wcag G18</code></pre>
      <p>These read bundled data. No command in this family opens a browser or makes a request.</p>
   </section>

   <section class="split panel" id="pattern">
      <h2>pattern</h2>
      <p>
         The ARIA Authoring Practices Guide publishes, for each of its 68 examples, a keyboard
         support table and a role, property, state, and tabindex table. This family reads those
         tables, and checks a live page against one.
      </p>
      <div class="route-list">
         <div class="route-row"><code>pattern</code><span>Every pattern with its example count. Same as <code>pattern list</code>.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>pattern &lt;name&gt;</code><span>One pattern by id such as <code>combobox</code>, with the guide's own text for it, section by section, and the examples the guide publishes for it. Or one example by id such as <code>combobox-select-only</code>, with its keyboard and attribute tables. <code>--section about</code>, <code>keyboard</code>, <code>attributes</code>, or <code>examples</code> narrows the output.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>pattern role &lt;role&gt;</code><span>The examples the APG example index files under one ARIA role.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>pattern attribute &lt;attribute&gt;</code><span>The examples the index files under one property or state, such as <code>aria-expanded</code>.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>pattern check &lt;target&gt;</code><span>Press every key one example declares against a live widget, and check its documented attributes. <code>--pattern</code> and <code>--selector</code> are both required. <code>--click</code> opens a widget the page renders only after a click, such as a dialog behind its trigger. <code>--table</code> and <code>--setup</code> reach a state the first keyboard table does not cover.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>pattern record &lt;target&gt;</code><span>Record what you decided about one row the check could not decide. <code>--pattern</code>, <code>--row</code>, <code>--outcome</code>, and <code>--selector</code> are required. Pass the same <code>--click</code> the check used. Supply <code>--run</code>, <code>--provenance</code>, and the current <code>--subject-hash</code> for a scoped assessment. A note alone stays unverified. Keyboard rows require typed keypress evidence for a documented key; attribute rows require observations of the widget's role or attribute.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>pattern pending &lt;target&gt;</code><span>The rows still awaiting verified evidence. Supply <code>--run</code>, <code>--pointer</code>, and <code>--subject-hash</code> to match the observed widget. Every queued state and environment must be assessed.</span></div>
      </div>
      <pre><code>a1 pattern combobox
a1 pattern combobox-select-only --section keyboard
a1 pattern windowsplitter --section keyboard
a1 pattern role combobox
a1 pattern attribute aria-expanded
a1 pattern check http://localhost:3000 --pattern combobox-select-only --selector '#fruit'
a1 pattern check http://localhost:3000 --pattern dialog --selector '[role="dialog"]' --click '#open-settings'
a1 pattern pending http://localhost:3000 --pattern combobox-select-only</code></pre>
      <p>
         Everything but <code>pattern check</code> reads bundled data. <code>pattern check</code>
         exits 4 on two findings and no others: a key the guide declares that changed nothing,
         and an attribute pointing at an id the document does not have. Every other key is
         printed with what changed next to the guide's own description of what should have
         changed, because whether the right thing happened is a judgment the tool does not make.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="split panel" id="axe">
      <div class="stack">
         <div class="panel-head">
            <p class="panel-kicker">axe</p>
            <h2>a1 axe</h2>
         </div>
         <div class="route-list">
            <div class="route-row"><code>&lt;targets...&gt;</code><span>One or more http(s) URLs or file paths, or <code>-</code> for HTML on stdin. Multiple targets print one report each.</span></div>
            <div class="route-row"><code>--html &lt;markup&gt;</code><span>Load inline HTML instead of the positional target.</span></div>
            <div class="route-row"><code>--timeout &lt;ms&gt;</code><span>Timeout for loading the target, in milliseconds. Defaults to 10000.</span></div>
            <div class="route-row"><code>--level &lt;level&gt;</code><span>Run the rules mapped to one WCAG level and every level below it.</span></div>
            <div class="route-row"><code>--criterion &lt;criterion&gt;</code><span>Run the rules mapped to one criterion, by ID or slug.</span></div>
            <div class="route-row"><code>--rule &lt;ruleId...&gt;</code><span>Run named axe rules. Repeat the flag for more than one.</span></div>
            <div class="route-row"><code>--selector &lt;css&gt;</code><span>Scope the scan to elements matching this CSS selector.</span></div>
            <div class="route-row"><code>--exclude &lt;css&gt;</code><span>Exclude elements matching this CSS selector from the scan.</span></div>
            <div class="route-row"><code>--wait-for &lt;css&gt;</code><span>Wait for an element matching this CSS selector before scanning.</span></div>
            <div class="route-row"><code>--click &lt;selector&gt;</code><span>Click the one element this selector matches after the page loads, before the scan. Reaches a dialog or a menu the page renders only after a click.</span></div>
            <div class="route-row"><code>--viewport &lt;WxH&gt;</code><span>Set the browser viewport, for example <code>1280x800</code>.</span></div>
            <div class="route-row"><code>--header &lt;header...&gt;</code><span>Repeatable. Add a request header, as <code>Name: value</code>.</span></div>
            <div class="route-row"><code>--cookie &lt;cookie...&gt;</code><span>Repeatable. Add a cookie, as <code>name=value</code>.</span></div>
            <div class="route-row"><code>--fail-on &lt;impact&gt;</code><span>Only count violations at or above this impact toward the exit code: minor, moderate, serious, or critical. Defaults to any violation.</span></div>
            <div class="route-row"><code>--baseline &lt;file&gt;</code><span>JSON file of accepted findings, keyed by rule id and node target, that do not count toward the exit code.</span></div>
            <div class="route-row"><code>--update-baseline</code><span>Write the current violations to <code>--baseline</code> instead of asserting against it.</span></div>
            <div class="route-row"><code>--format &lt;format&gt;</code><span>text, json, sarif, or earl. Defaults to text, or json with <code>--json</code>.</span></div>
            <div class="route-row"><code>--out &lt;file&gt;</code><span>Write the report to this file instead of stdout.</span></div>
         </div>
         <pre><code>a1 axe http://localhost:3000 --level AA --json
a1 axe http://localhost:3000 --criterion 1.4.3 --json
a1 axe src/components/Dialog.html --rule color-contrast --rule link-name --json
echo '&lt;button&gt;&lt;/button&gt;' | a1 axe -
a1 axe --html '&lt;img src=x&gt;'
a1 axe http://localhost:3000 --selector '#app' --exclude '.ads'
a1 axe http://localhost:3000 --fail-on critical --json
a1 axe http://localhost:3000 --baseline known-findings.json --update-baseline
a1 axe http://localhost:3000 --format sarif --out results.sarif
a1 axe http://localhost:3000 --format earl --out report.earl.json
a1 axe http://localhost:3000/a http://localhost:3000/b --json</code></pre>
         <p>
            With no selector, the run covers every rule mapped in the WCAG version. The result has
            <code>violations</code>, <code>passes</code>, <code>incomplete</code>, and
            <code>inapplicable</code>, plus the resolved rule list under <code>selection</code>.
         </p>
         <p>
            A file target loads through <code>file://</code>. axe never fetches a URL target's
            HTML itself, since Playwright loads the page directly.
         </p>
         <p>
            With more than one target, the JSON result's <code>targets</code> array has one
            report per target, and text mode prints one report after another.
         </p>
         <p>
            <code>--format sarif</code> writes a SARIF 2.1.0 log with one rule descriptor per
            unique axe rule and one result per violating node, merged across every target when
            more than one is given. It bypasses the envelope entirely, so combine it with
            <code>--out</code> to feed a static analysis tool such as GitHub code scanning.
         </p>
         <p>
            <code>--format earl</code> writes an
            <a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/EARL10-Schema/">EARL 1.0</a> report as JSON-LD, the
            format the W3C reads. The graph is a flat list of assertions, one per failing
            element, each naming the axe rule that found it and the success criteria that fail
            with it. Violations become <code>earl:failed</code>, passes
            <code>earl:passed</code>, incomplete results <code>earl:cantTell</code>, and a run
            that matched no rule at all becomes a single <code>earl:untested</code> assertion
            rather than an empty report. Like SARIF it bypasses the envelope, so pair it with
            <code>--out</code>.
         </p>
         <p>
            axe exits 4 when a violation remains after <code>--fail-on</code> and
            <code>--baseline</code> filtering, and 0 otherwise. The JSON envelope's
            <code>ok</code> field stays <code>true</code> either way. The exit code is the
            verdict, and the envelope repeats it under <code>result.verdict</code>.
         </p>
         <p>
            Run with <code>--update-baseline</code> once to accept the current findings, then
            commit the baseline file so CI only fails on new ones. See
            <a href="/guides/violations">understand a violation</a> for what impact means and how
            to get from a violation to a fix, and
            <a href="/guides/scripting">script the CLI</a> for the exit codes and the envelope
            shape.
         </p>
      </div>
      <div class="split-aside">
         <div class="tag">Thin pages</div>
         <p class="aside-note">
            If the page has fewer than 10 elements and under 50 characters of text, the result adds a
            warning that a single-page app may not have mounted.
         </p>
      </div>
   </section>

   <section class="split panel" id="tree">
      <div class="stack">
         <div class="panel-head">
            <p class="panel-kicker">tree</p>
            <h2>a1 tree</h2>
         </div>
         <div class="route-list">
            <div class="route-row"><code>&lt;target&gt;</code><span>An http(s) URL, a file path, - for HTML on stdin, or --html.</span></div>
            <div class="route-row"><code>--click &lt;selector&gt;</code><span>Click the one element this selector matches after the page loads, before reading the tree.</span></div>
            <div class="route-row"><code>--role &lt;role&gt;</code><span>Keep only nodes with this role, and their ancestors.</span></div>
            <div class="route-row"><code>--name &lt;text&gt;</code><span>Keep only nodes whose accessible name contains this text, and their ancestors.</span></div>
         </div>
         <pre><code>a1 tree http://localhost:3000
a1 tree http://localhost:3000 --role link
a1 tree http://localhost:3000 --name "Sign in" --json</code></pre>
         <p>
            <code>tree</code> reads Playwright's <code>ariaSnapshot()</code> for the target: the
            role and name tree a screen reader sees, including JavaScript-rendered content, with
            no heuristics. Text output is the YAML Playwright produces.
         </p>
         <p>
            <code>--json</code> parses that YAML into <code>result.nodes</code>, an array of
            <code>{'{'} role, name, level, children {'}'}</code> objects.
         </p>
         <p>
            <code>--role</code> and <code>--name</code> keep a matching node together with every
            ancestor needed to reach it, so the tree's shape stays intact.
         </p>
      </div>
      <div class="split-aside">
         <div class="tag">No heuristics</div>
         <p class="aside-note">
            <code>tree</code> runs the page in a real browser, so it sees content a client-side
            framework renders after load.
         </p>
      </div>
   </section>

   <section class="split panel" id="audit">
      <div class="stack">
         <div class="panel-head">
            <p class="panel-kicker">Page observations</p>
            <h2>a1 audit</h2>
         </div>
         <div class="route-list">
            <div class="route-row"><code>&lt;target&gt;</code><span>An http(s) URL, a file path, - for HTML on stdin, or --html.</span></div>
            <div class="route-row"><code>--fail-on &lt;impact&gt;</code><span>Only count axe violations at or above this impact toward the exit code: minor, moderate, serious, or critical. Defaults to any violation.</span></div>
            <div class="route-row"><code>--baseline &lt;file&gt;</code><span>JSON file of accepted axe findings that do not count toward the exit code.</span></div>
            <div class="route-row"><code>--update-baseline</code><span>Write the current axe violations to <code>--baseline</code> instead of asserting against it.</span></div>
            <div class="route-row"><code>--results &lt;file&gt;</code><span>Read recorded manual results from here. Defaults to <code>.a11ied/evidence.jsonl</code>, or <code>$A11IED_EVIDENCE</code>.</span></div>
            <div class="route-row"><code>--format &lt;format&gt;</code><span>text, json, or earl. Defaults to text, or json with <code>--json</code>.</span></div>
            <div class="route-row"><code>--out &lt;file&gt;</code><span>Write the report to this file instead of stdout.</span></div>
         </div>
         <pre><code>a1 audit http://localhost:3000 --json
a1 audit http://localhost:3000 --fail-on critical
a1 audit http://localhost:3000 --format earl --out report.earl.json
a1 audit --html '&lt;h1&gt;Hi&lt;/h1&gt;'</code></pre>
         <p>
            <code>audit</code> runs axe against every rule mapped in the WCAG version, reads the
            accessibility tree, and derives applicability from the target's HTML, all against one
            resolved target. The result includes these sections:
         </p>
         <div class="route-list">
            <div class="route-row"><code>axe</code><span>The full axe run.</span></div>
            <div class="route-row"><code>tree</code><span>Page title, first heading, and landmark, heading, link, button, and form counts.</span></div>
            <div class="route-row"><code>applicability</code><span>The signal-backed matrix. See <a href="/relevant-criteria">applicability</a> for how the states are decided.</span></div>
            <div class="route-row"><code>criteria</code><span>A per-criterion rollup of axe verdict, applicability state, coverage state, evidence mode, and whether the criterion is still <code>pending</code> a person.</span></div>
            <div class="route-row"><code>recorded</code><span>The results a person or an agent recorded for this target.</span></div>
         </div>
         <p>
            <code>result.nextCommands</code> lists <code>a1 wcag rule</code> calls for every
            failing axe rule.
         </p>
         <p>
            <code>audit</code> exits 4 when an axe violation remains after
            <code>--fail-on</code> and <code>--baseline</code> filtering, and 0 otherwise, the same
            verdict rules as <code>a1 axe</code>.
         </p>
         <h3>Checks a11ied cannot automate</h3>
         <p>
            Axe checks specific conditions within selected criteria. Assessment procedures
            cover the remaining behavior and content requirements. An agent records observed
            results through these commands.
         </p>
         <div class="route-list">
            <div class="route-row"><code>a1 audit pending &lt;target&gt;</code><span>List criteria with remaining manual procedures. Each returned procedure needs its own recorded result. Reads WCAG data and the evidence file without opening a browser.</span></div>
            <div class="route-row"><code>a1 audit record &lt;target&gt;</code><span>Record one outcome. Takes <code>--criterion</code>, <code>--outcome</code>, and optionally <code>--pointer</code>, <code>--note</code>, <code>--procedure</code>, <code>--mode</code>, <code>--by</code>, <code>--run</code>, and <code>--provenance</code>. <code>--finding &lt;file&gt;</code> accepts a JSON title, userImpact, optional remediation, and optional impact. Notes without current run and artifact provenance remain unverified and do not complete a criterion.</span></div>
            <div class="route-row"><code>a1 audit clear &lt;target&gt;</code><span>Drop recorded results for one target.</span></div>
         </div>
         <pre><code>a1 audit pending http://localhost:3000 --level AA
a1 audit record http://localhost:3000 --criterion 2.4.4 --outcome failed \
   --pointer 'nav &gt; a:nth-child(3)' --note "Four links read 'Learn more'."
a1 audit http://localhost:3000 --format earl --out report.earl.json</code></pre>
         <p>
         Pass <code>--results &lt;run-dir&gt;/evidence.jsonl</code> on every audit, pending, and
         record call in a site audit. Resume with the same file; use a fresh file for a new
         run. A passing procedure does not erase a failure recorded for another procedure.
      </p>
      <p>
            <code>--outcome</code> takes passed, failed, cantTell, or inapplicable. Results go to
            <code>.a11ied/evidence.jsonl</code>, one JSON object per line, so several agent calls
            can record at once. Recording the same procedure and element again replaces its earlier result.
            Recording a result never stores it under the axe procedure, so a person's judgment
            cannot appear in the report as an automated one.
         </p>
         <p>
            <code>--format earl</code> then writes one report holding both. A recorded result
            keeps <code>mode: earl:manual</code> or <code>earl:semiAutomatic</code>, so a reader
            can tell it from an axe result.
         </p>
      </div>
      <div class="split-aside">
         <div class="tag">One target, one report</div>
         <p class="aside-note">
            <code>audit</code> is the command to reach for first on a new page. Follow up with
            <code>a1 axe</code>, <code>a1 tree</code>, or <code>a1 wcag show</code> once you know
            what to look at.
         </p>
      </div>
   </section>

   <section class="panel" id="assessment">
      <h2>Agent-led assessment</h2>
      <p>
         <code>audit run</code> creates durable work for a host agent. It sends no UI input.
         The agent performs each procedure, saves observed evidence, and evaluates the result.
         The environment JSON records its observed platform, OS, capabilities, and limitations.
         Web scope defaults to site; <code>--app</code> selects an existing desktop app target and defaults to app scope.
      </p>
      <p>
         With <code>--inventory</code>, startup uses the discovered run ID and links the
         coordinator without editing JSON. Its default path is <code>run.json</code> beside
         the inventory. A compatible coordinator can be attached after interrupted startup;
         conflicting identity, profile, environment, policy, or inventory paths are rejected.
      </p>
      <p>
         Evidence provenance and non-image artifact envelopes include the claimed check's
         <code>attempts</code> value as <code>attempt</code>. A retry requires fresh proof for
         that generation, even after a clock change. Raw screenshots retain their PNG format;
         their provenance and companion artifacts identify the attempt.
      </p>
      <pre><code>a1 audit discover https://createdbyfireside.com --out .a11ied/fireside/inventory.json
a1 audit run https://createdbyfireside.com --environment environment.json \
  --inventory .a11ied/fireside/inventory.json --run .a11ied/fireside/run.json
a1 audit next .a11ied/fireside/run.json --json
a1 audit status .a11ied/fireside/run.json --limit 20 --json</code></pre>
      <div class="route-list">
         <div class="route-row"><code>run [url]</code><span>Requires <code>--environment</code>. Accepts <code>--app</code>, <code>--scope</code>, <code>--wcag</code>, <code>--level</code>, <code>--inventory</code>, <code>--run</code>, <code>--id</code>, and <code>--policy</code>. Policy JSON declares allowed origins and approved submission or destructive actions; both action flags default to disabled.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>state, journey, queue &lt;run&gt;</code><span>Register an observed state, ordered journey, or explicit obligation with <code>--input &lt;file&gt;</code>. Use <code>-</code> for stdin. Responses return the saved identity and revision even beyond the first status page.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>next &lt;run&gt;</code><span>Claim one check with its procedure and observed scope context. Execute its setup and actions, preserve evidence, and supply the required agent judgment. A changed claim is rejected before any UI input.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>evaluate &lt;run&gt;</code><span>Requires <code>--check</code>, <code>--outcome</code>, and saved <code>--evidence &lt;id...&gt;</code> from <code>audit record --run</code>. Missing, mismatched, or stale proof is rejected.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>block, resume &lt;run&gt;</code><span>Block a check with <code>--check</code> and <code>--reason</code>. Resume preserves interrupted work as blocked. After restoring a safe state, pass explicit <code>--retry &lt;check...&gt;</code>; completed assessments cannot be replayed.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>status, finalize &lt;run&gt;</code><span>Status revalidates coverage, artifacts, and scope. Lists use <code>--offset</code> and <code>--limit</code> (1 to 100; default 20). Finalize requires complete validated coverage; <code>--partial</code> returns unresolved work and keeps the run active.</span></div>
      </div>
      <p>
         Finalization updates assessment status. Report building remains a separate operation
         over discovery and page artifacts. Native reader binding and complete installed audits
         still require their own runtime verification.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="panel" id="doctor">
      <div class="panel-head">
         <p class="panel-kicker">Runtime</p>
         <h2>a1 doctor, a1 mcp, and a1 help-all</h2>
      </div>
      <pre><code>a1 doctor
a1 doctor --json
a1 doctor --task scan --strict
a1 doctor --task audit --sr voiceover --strict
a1 doctor --task reader --sr nvda --recording
a1 setup
a1 setup --json
a1 setup --skip-setup
a1 mcp
a1 help-all</code></pre>
      <p>
         <code>doctor</code> checks this machine and leads with a verdict: <code>✓</code> ready, or
         <code>✗</code> with the number of setup steps still needed. Action items with the exact
         commands come first, then each target with its individual checks, the browser a11ied will
         automate, and the a11ied, Node, npm, and host versions at the bottom.
      </p>
      <p>
         Use <code>--task scan</code> for browser scans, <code>--task reader</code> for reader sessions,
         or <code>--task audit</code> for desktop audit prerequisites. Use <code>--sr voiceover</code>
         on macOS or <code>--sr nvda</code> on Windows to check one reader. A virtual reader cannot
         satisfy audit prerequisites. These checks do not establish audit coverage.
      </p>
      <p>
         Recording permission is checked only with <code>--recording</code>. On macOS this captures
         a temporary video from the current host app and deletes it. Scans do not check reader settings.
         The exit code is 0 unless you pass <code>--strict</code>, which exits with 3 while a
         required step is missing. <code>--json</code> wraps the report in the standard envelope,
         under <code>result</code>.
      </p>
      <p>
         <code>setup</code> runs the Guidepup commands for this host in order,
         <code>npx -y @guidepup/setup setup</code> on macOS and then
         <code>npx -y @guidepup/setup install</code> on macOS and Windows, from the directory
         where <code>@guidepup/guidepup</code> resolves. Each command keeps the terminal while it
         runs, and setup stops at the first failure.
      </p>
      <p>
         It then prints the <code>doctor</code> report and exits with 3 when a required step is
         still missing. <code>--skip-setup</code> and <code>--skip-install</code> drop one of the
         two steps. <code>--json</code> replaces the progress lines with an envelope that includes
         each step's exit code and the closing doctor report under <code>result</code>.
      </p>
      <p>
         <code>mcp</code> takes no options and speaks stdio. <code>help-all</code> prints every
         command, option, and the full named-command list in one response, which is what to give an
         agent instead of a sequence of help calls.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="field-note" id="browser">
      <div class="panel-head">
         <p class="panel-kicker">Browsers</p>
         <h2>Browser selection</h2>
      </div>
      <p>
         The policy is system browser first. <code>a1 axe</code> prefers an installed
         Chrome, Edge, Brave, or Chromium over Playwright's managed Chromium. If neither is
         present, the command tells you to run <code>npx playwright install chromium</code>.
      </p>
      <p>
         <code>a1 doctor --json</code> lists every candidate it found under
         <code>result.browserAutomation.candidates</code>, with the executable path for each.
      </p>
   </section>
</DocShell>
